/// Chapter 34 of the guide, written from md-docs/recipes/deploying.md by
/// scripts/docsite/guide.py; edit the guide, not this file.
page DeployingGuide(path: "/docs/guide/deploying", title: "Deploying", description: "Deploy a build to GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages, nginx, Apache, Caddy or Docker, with cache and security headers.", layout: DocsShell("Deploying", kind: .howto, chapter: t("nav.group.ship"), blurb: "The build is a folder of static files. Put it on GitHub Pages, Netlify, Vercel, Cloudflare Pages, a server of your own, or a container.", source: "md-docs/recipes/deploying.md", sections: [Anchor(id: "before-you-start", label: "Before you start"), Anchor(id: "install-wf-on-a-build-machine", label: "Install wf on a build machine"), Anchor(id: "deploy-to-github-pages", label: "Deploy to GitHub Pages"), Anchor(id: "deploy-to-netlify", label: "Deploy to Netlify"), Anchor(id: "deploy-to-cloudflare-pages", label: "Deploy to Cloudflare Pages"), Anchor(id: "deploy-to-vercel", label: "Deploy to Vercel"), Anchor(id: "serve-with-nginx", label: "Serve with nginx"), Anchor(id: "serve-with-apache", label: "Serve with Apache"), Anchor(id: "serve-with-caddy", label: "Serve with Caddy"), Anchor(id: "build-a-docker-image", label: "Build a Docker image"), Anchor(id: "set-cache-headers", label: "Set cache headers"), Anchor(id: "set-security-headers", label: "Set security headers"), Anchor(id: "serve-an-api-on-the-same-origin", label: "Serve an API on the same origin"), Anchor(id: "check-the-deploy", label: "Check the deploy")], prevTo: "/docs/guide/static-and-spa", prevLabel: t("ch.33"), nextTo: "/docs/guide/environments", nextLabel: t("ch.35"))) {
    Stack(gap: .lg) {
        Markdown("This chapter shows you how to put a WebFluent site on the web. `wf build` writes one folder, `build/`, and that folder is the whole site: any host that serves static files can serve it, and there is no server process to run.\n\nThe folder holds HTML, CSS, JavaScript, images, `sitemap.xml`, `robots.txt`, `_headers` (the security headers, for hosts that read the file), and a `.gz` file beside every text file. The same folder is also what a desktop shell shows: [Desktop apps](/docs/guide/desktop) makes a Tauri or an Electron app of it.")

        Heading("Before you start", id: "before-you-start").h2

        Markdown("Do these once, before the first deploy:\n\n1. Set `meta.site_url` in `webfluent.app.json` to the address the site will have. If the site lives under a sub-path (`/docs`), also set `build.base_path`.\n2. Decide between a static build and a single-page build ([Static sites and SPAs](/docs/guide/static-and-spa)). A single-page build needs the host to answer every path with `index.html`.\n3. Build the site and load it in a real browser:\n\n   ```bash wf build wf verify ```\n\n   `wf verify` loads every page in headless Chrome and fails on a script error, a failed request or a blank page.\n\nA static build writes each page as its own `index.html` (`/contact` is `contact/index.html`). A page whose `path` is `*` is written to the root as `404.html`, which is the file most static hosts serve for a path they have no file for.")

        Heading("Install wf on a build machine", id: "install-wf-on-a-build-machine").h2

        Markdown("To build on the host's build machine, install `wf` and run the build. Every recipe in this chapter uses these two lines:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash"),
                Line(command: "~/.webfluent/bin/wf build")
            ])

        Markdown("Pin a version so that a new release cannot change a deploy without you knowing. Set `WF_VERSION` for the install script:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | WF_VERSION=v6.0.0 bash")
            ])

        Markdown("You can also build on your own machine and upload `build/`. It is only files.")

        Heading("Deploy to GitHub Pages", id: "deploy-to-github-pages").h2

        Markdown("GitHub serves a project site under `https://<you>.github.io/<repo>/`, so the build must know that sub-path.\n\n1. In `webfluent.app.json`, set `\"base_path\": \"/<repo>\"` in `build` and `\"site_url\": \"https://<you>.github.io/<repo>\"` in `meta`.\n2. GitHub Pages answers `/contact` with a `301` redirect to `/contact/`. To make the canonical address of every page one it serves without a redirect, also set `\"clean_urls\": \"file\"` (or `\"directory\"`) in `build` ([Content](/docs/guide/content#keep-addresses-from-redirecting)).\n3. Add `.github/workflows/pages.yml`:\n\n   ```yaml name: Deploy on: push: branches: [main] permissions: contents: read pages: write id-token: write jobs: deploy: runs-on: ubuntu-latest environment: name: github-pages url: $\{\{ steps.deployment.outputs.page_url \}\} steps:\n         - uses: actions/checkout@v4\n         - run: curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | WF_VERSION=v6.0.0 bash\n         - run: ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build\n         - uses: actions/upload-pages-artifact@v3 with: path: build\n         - id: deployment uses: actions/deploy-pages@v4 ```\n\n4. In the repository's **Settings → Pages**, set the source to **GitHub Actions**.\n5. Push to `main`.\n\nGitHub Pages serves `404.html` for a path it has no file for. It ignores `_headers`, but the Content-Security-Policy still ships as a `<meta>` tag in every page.\n\n**Verify it:** open the URL the workflow's deploy step prints, then open a page other than the home page directly and reload it. With `clean_urls` set, the address stays as you typed it, with no redirect.")

        Heading("Deploy to Netlify", id: "deploy-to-netlify").h2

        Markdown("1. Add `netlify.toml` at the project's root:\n\n   ```toml [build] command = \"curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash && ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build\" publish = \"build\" ```\n\n2. For a single-page build, add `public/_redirects` with this line. The build copies it to the root of `build/`:\n\n   ```text /*  /index.html  200 ```\n\n3. Connect the repository in Netlify, or push to the branch it deploys.\n\nNetlify reads `_headers` from the build, so the security headers apply with no further setting.\n\n**Verify it:** in the browser's network panel, select the page's document request. Its response headers include `Content-Security-Policy` and `X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff`, which came from `_headers`.")

        Heading("Deploy to Cloudflare Pages", id: "deploy-to-cloudflare-pages").h2

        Markdown("1. In the Cloudflare Pages project, set the build command to the one in the Netlify recipe above: `curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash && ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build`.\n2. Set the build output directory to `build`.\n3. For a single-page build, add `public/_redirects` as for Netlify.\n\nCloudflare Pages reads `_headers` and `_redirects` the same way Netlify does.\n\n**Verify it:** check the response headers in the network panel, as for Netlify, and open a deep link directly.")

        Heading("Deploy to Vercel", id: "deploy-to-vercel").h2

        Markdown("1. Add `vercel.json` at the project's root:\n\n   ```json \{ \"buildCommand\": \"curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash && ~/.webfluent/bin/wf build\", \"outputDirectory\": \"build\", \"headers\": [ \{ \"source\": \"/(.*)\", \"headers\": [ \{ \"key\": \"X-Content-Type-Options\", \"value\": \"nosniff\" \}, \{ \"key\": \"Referrer-Policy\", \"value\": \"strict-origin-when-cross-origin\" \}, \{ \"key\": \"X-Frame-Options\", \"value\": \"DENY\" \} ] \} ] \} ```\n\n2. Vercel does not read `_headers`. Copy every header the build wrote in `build/_headers` into `headers`, including the `Content-Security-Policy` line.\n3. For a single-page build, add `\"rewrites\": [\{ \"source\": \"/(.*)\", \"destination\": \"/index.html\" \}]`.\n\n**Verify it:** compare the response headers in the network panel with `build/_headers`. Each header in the file should be there.")

        Heading("Serve with nginx", id: "serve-with-nginx").h2

        Markdown("1. Add a `server` block. Pick the `try_files` line for your kind of build:\n\n   ```nginx server \{ listen 443 ssl http2; server_name example.com; root /var/www/site;\n\n       gzip_static on;                          # serve the .gz files the build wrote\n\n       location / \{ try_files $uri $uri/ $uri.html =404; # a static build # try_files $uri $uri/ /index.html;  # a single-page build \} error_page 404 /404.html;\n\n       location = /sw.js \{ add_header Cache-Control \"no-cache\"; \} location ~* \\.(webp|avif|jpg|jpeg|png)$ \{ add_header Cache-Control \"public, max-age=31536000, immutable\"; \}\n\n       add_header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff always; add_header Referrer-Policy strict-origin-when-cross-origin always; add_header X-Frame-Options DENY always; # add_header Content-Security-Policy \"…as in build/_headers…\" always; \} ```\n\n2. Upload the build with any tool. For example:\n\n   ```bash rsync -a --delete build/ server:/var/www/site/ ```\n\n3. Reload nginx.\n\n**Verify it:** `curl -I` a page and check the headers. Ask for a compressed copy and check that nginx sends the build's `.gz` file:")

        Terminal(lines: [
                Line(command: "curl -sI -H \"Accept-Encoding: gzip\" https://example.com/app.js")
            ])

        Markdown("The response has `Content-Encoding: gzip`.")

        Heading("Serve with Apache", id: "serve-with-apache").h2

        Markdown("1. Put this in `public/.htaccess`. The build copies it to the root of `build/`:\n\n   ```apache Options -MultiViews RewriteEngine On # A single-page build: # RewriteCond %\{REQUEST_FILENAME\} !-f # RewriteRule ^ /index.html [L] ErrorDocument 404 /404.html Header always set X-Content-Type-Options \"nosniff\" Header always set X-Frame-Options \"DENY\" ```\n\n2. For a single-page build, uncomment the two rewrite lines.\n3. Compress the text files. Either turn on `mod_deflate`, which compresses on the way out, or serve the build's `.gz` files with `MultiViews` (in place of `-MultiViews`) and `AddEncoding gzip .gz`.\n4. Upload `build/` to the site's document root.\n\n**Verify it:** `curl -I` a page and check for `X-Content-Type-Options` and `X-Frame-Options`. Open a path that does not exist and check that you get the site's `404.html`.")

        Heading("Serve with Caddy", id: "serve-with-caddy").h2

        Markdown("1. Add a site block to the `Caddyfile`:\n\n   ```text example.com \{ root * /var/www/site encode gzip file_server \{ precompressed gzip \} try_files \{path\} \{path\}/ \{path\}.html /index.html header X-Content-Type-Options nosniff \} ```\n\n   `precompressed gzip` serves the build's `.gz` files. The last entry of `try_files`, `/index.html`, is the single-page fallback; remove it for a static build, so an unknown path is a 404.\n2. Upload `build/` to `/var/www/site` and reload Caddy.\n\n**Verify it:** open a deep link directly and reload it, then check the headers with `curl -I`.")

        Heading("Build a Docker image", id: "build-a-docker-image").h2

        Markdown("1. Add a `Dockerfile`. The first stage installs `wf` and builds the site; the second serves it with nginx:\n\n   ```dockerfile FROM debian:stable-slim AS build RUN apt-get update && apt-get install -y curl ca-certificates RUN curl -sSL https://raw.githubusercontent.com/monzeromer-lab/WebFluent/master/install.sh | bash WORKDIR /site COPY . . RUN /root/.webfluent/bin/wf build\n\n   FROM nginx:alpine COPY --from=build /site/build /usr/share/nginx/html ```\n\n2. To use the settings from [Serve with nginx](#serve-with-nginx), copy them into the second stage as `/etc/nginx/conf.d/default.conf`. When TLS ends in front of the container, write `listen 80;` in place of the `listen 443 ssl http2;` line.\n3. Build and run the image:\n\n   ```bash docker build -t site . docker run -p 8080:80 site ```\n\n**Verify it:** open `http://localhost:8080` and a deep link, and check the headers with `curl -I http://localhost:8080/`.")

        Heading("Set cache headers", id: "set-cache-headers").h2

        Markdown("To let browsers keep files without serving a stale site, set `Cache-Control` by the kind of file:")

        Table(caption: "Set cache headers") {
            Table.Head {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell("Files").header
                    Table.Cell("Cache-Control").header
                    Table.Cell("Why").header
                }
            }
            Table.Body {
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("HTML, `app.js`, `pages/*`, `styles.css`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`no-cache` (revalidate every time)") }
                    Table.Cell("their names do not change between builds")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`sw.js`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`no-cache`") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("a stale worker holds a stale site ([Offline](/docs/guide/offline))") }
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("resized images (`hero.<hash>.960.webp`)") }
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`public, max-age=31536000, immutable`") }
                    Table.Cell("the name changes when the file does")
                }
                Table.Row {
                    Table.Cell { Markdown("`public/` files") }
                    Table.Cell("your choice")
                    Table.Cell("they keep the names you gave them")
                }
            }
        }

        Markdown("`no-cache` still lets the browser keep a copy. The browser asks the server each time, and a `304 Not Modified` answer means it does not download the file again.")

        Heading("Set security headers", id: "set-security-headers").h2

        Markdown("With `build.csp` on, every page carries its Content-Security-Policy as a `<meta>` tag. `build/_headers` holds the same policy, plus `X-Content-Type-Options`, `Referrer-Policy`, `X-Frame-Options` and the policy's `frame-ancestors`, which only work as real HTTP headers. This is the file a build writes:")

        CodeBlock(#"/*
  Content-Security-Policy: default-src 'self'; script-src 'self'; style-src 'self'; img-src 'self' data:; font-src 'self'; object-src 'none'; base-uri 'none'; frame-ancestors 'none'; form-action 'self'
  X-Content-Type-Options: nosniff
  Referrer-Policy: strict-origin-when-cross-origin
  X-Frame-Options: DENY"#, title: "text")

        Markdown("Netlify and Cloudflare Pages read the file. On any other host, set the same headers as the recipes above show. Serve the site over HTTPS, and turn on HSTS once you are sure HTTPS works everywhere. [Security](/docs/guide/security#before-you-deploy) has the full checklist.")

        Heading("Serve an API on the same origin", id: "serve-an-api-on-the-same-origin").h2

        Markdown("To call a backend without CORS, serve the site and the backend from one origin: the site at `/`, the backend at `/api`. A session cookie then works with no extra setting. In nginx:")

        CodeBlock(#"location /api/ {
    proxy_pass http://127.0.0.1:8080/;
}"#, title: "nginx")

        Markdown("Then `api Backend(base: \"/api\")` needs nothing else. Netlify, Vercel and Cloudflare Pages do the same with a rewrite to an external URL.")

        Heading("Check the deploy", id: "check-the-deploy").h2

        Markdown("After each deploy, check these:\n\n- Open a deep link directly and reload it. A 404 means the host does not fall back to `index.html` (a single-page build), or `base_path` is wrong.\n- View a page's source. In a static build, the page's text is in the HTML.\n- Check the response headers in the browser's network panel.\n- Run a link-preview debugger on a page, and submit `sitemap.xml` to the search engines' consoles.")
    }
}
